\section{File names}{

Files from Plank are named \code{name.plank.html} by convention: the \code{.html}
ending makes the file open in a browser on any computer, and the \code{.plank}
before it makes plank files easy to find (search for \code{.plank.html}) and
tell apart from other web pages. A \code{file} without an extension, or ending
in \code{.plank} (or the older \code{.pqv}), gets \code{.plank.html} (or \code{.html}) added. A file name that does not
end in \code{.html} or \code{.htm} gives a warning, because browsers show such a
file as plain text instead of opening the viewer.
